<script lang="ts">export let version = 'v1';</script>

<div class="section-heading"><div><h1>Understanding the scores</h1><p>What each measure tells you, and how it is calculated.</p></div><span class="badge">Alignment · {version}</span></div>
<article class="metrics">
  <p>A system receives recitation audio and returns timed segments labelled with Quran word references. We compare those claims against a reviewed word-level timeline. Repeated words appear in that timeline every time they are recited.</p>
  <div class="metric-index"><a href="#words">Words</a><a href="#clean">Clean segments</a><a href="#repeats">Repeats</a><a href="#context">Granularity & runtime</a><a href="#confidence">Confidence</a></div>
  <section id="words"><h2>Words F1</h2><p>Did the system find the recited words, and are its claims correct? F1 balances both. A system cannot score well just by claiming everything, or by finding only a few easy words.</p>
  <dl><dt>Words found <span>(recall)</span></dt><dd>Of all the words recited, the share the system found in the right segments.</dd><dt>Words correct <span>(precision)</span></dt><dd>Of all the words the system claimed, the share actually recited in those segments.</dd></dl>
  <div class="formula">Found = matched words / recited words<br/>Correct = matched words / claimed words<br/>F1 = 2 × Found × Correct / (Found + Correct)</div>
  <p class="example">Example: 80 matches out of 100 recited words and 90 claimed words gives 80% found, 88.9% correct, and 84.2% F1.</p>
  <details><summary>How words are assigned and matched</summary><p>A word belongs to the first submitted segment containing its midpoint, including endpoints. Within that segment, a longest common subsequence matches the claimed words to the recited words in order. Each claim matches at most one recited instance; ties prefer earlier instances. Uncovered words and Quran audio labelled as null remain unfound.</p><p>A reference is a forward word span within one surah. A single span cannot claim two takes of a repeated passage; separate segments are needed. Correct word counts are independent of segmentation granularity.</p><p>F1 is zero when only one side has anything to score or either defined component is zero. It is undefined only when neither side has anything to score.</p></details></section>
  <section id="clean"><h2>Clean segments</h2><p>The share of Quran-labelled segments that are correct as a whole, with usable boundaries. For captions, this is the share that can be shown as supplied. Read it alongside Words / segment: a long caption has more opportunities for an error.</p><div class="formula">Clean segments = clean Quran segments / all Quran segments</div>
  <p>A clean segment has exactly the claimed words, covers their audio, and avoids excessive silence, neighbouring words, or other speech.</p>
  <details><summary>Exact boundary rules</summary><p>Let a and b be the first matched word's start and last matched word's end. Let p be the previous outside word's end, and n the next outside word's start. Drop a neighbour bound when that neighbour is absent. Times are seconds.</p><div class="formula">max(a − 2, p − 0.5) ≤ segment start ≤ a + 0.5<br/>b − 0.5 ≤ segment end ≤ min(b + 2, n + 0.5)<br/>segment duration ≥ (b − a) / 2</div><p>The attributed, claimed, and matched word counts must all be equal, including formulas. Total overlap with annotated non-Quran speech must be at most 0.5 seconds. Formula and null segments are excluded from this metric.</p></details></section>
  <section id="repeats"><h2>Repeats F1</h2><p>Does the system notice when the reciter goes back and recites words again? A repeat is a return to the same or an earlier word within a surah.</p><dl><dt>Repeats caught <span>(recall)</span></dt><dd>The share of real repeat events the system detected.</dd><dt>Repeats real <span>(precision)</span></dt><dd>The share of the system's detected repeat events that correspond to real ones.</dd></dl><div class="formula">Caught = matched repeat events / actual repeat events<br/>Real = matched repeat events / predicted repeat events<br/>Repeats F1 = 2 × Caught × Real / (Caught + Real)</div>
  <details><summary>What counts as detecting a repeat?</summary><p>The system marks an event when a Quran segment starts at or before the last claimed word of its previous Quran segment in the same surah. Formula and null segments are skipped. The true restart word must be matched in that predicted-event segment. Each predicted event can catch at most one true event, choosing the earliest.</p><p>Surah changes reset the comparison. When neither side contains repeats, all three values are undefined. When only one side contains repeats, F1 is zero; a component whose denominator is zero is undefined. Restart distance is a diagnostic, not part of F1.</p></details></section>
  <section id="context"><h2>Granularity, speed, and controls</h2><h3>Words / segment</h3><p>The average number of claimed Quran words per Quran segment. This describes how finely the output is divided; it is not a quality score.</p><div class="formula">Words / segment = claimed Quran words / Quran segments</div><h3>Processing speed <span>optional</span></h3><p>The leaderboard shows how many seconds of audio are processed per second. A speed of 10× realtime means one minute of audio took six seconds to process. Higher is faster. The exact real-time factor (RTF) remains visible for technical comparison: 10× realtime equals RTF 0.1.</p><div class="formula">RTF = processing seconds / audio seconds<br/>Processing speed = 1 / RTF</div><p>Runtime is self-reported, and CPU/GPU and hardware details matter when comparing it. Report runtime for every recording or none.</p><h3>User-facing parameters</h3><p>A self-reported count of result-affecting controls available to the system's intended users, such as a silence threshold or a style setting. For a demo these are its user controls; for a package these are its documented, exposed options. Internal developer settings do not count. More controls can offer flexibility; fewer can make a system easier to use. This is context, not a score.</p></section>
  <section id="confidence"><h2>Consumer confidence <span>optional</span></h2><p>Confidence is evaluated once per Quran segment, matching what a user sees. Green means the system considers a segment safe to use, amber means review it, and red means it is probably wrong.</p><div class="formula">Green: confidence ≥ 0.80<br/>Amber: 0.60 ≤ confidence &lt; 0.80<br/>Red: confidence &lt; 0.60</div><h3>Trusted coverage</h3><p>The useful share of output that the system can safely mark green. Each incorrect green segment cancels four correct green segments, so reporting everything green cannot produce a good score unless it is reliable.</p><div class="formula">Trusted coverage = max(0, (correct green − 4 × incorrect green) / Quran segments)</div><h3>Unsafe green</h3><p>The share of green segments that are not clean. Lower is safer. A missing value means the system produced no green segments or did not report confidence.</p><div class="formula">Unsafe green = incorrect green / all green segments</div><details><summary>Detailed confidence diagnostics</summary><p>A clean segment has all the right words and usable boundaries under the same Clean segments rules above. Confidence must be supplied for every eligible Quran-span and Basmala segment or none, consistently across recordings.</p><p>The downloadable report includes counts and accuracy for all three tiers, safe green coverage, critical green claims over non-Quran audio, and segment-level Brier score, Brier skill, AUROC, calibration error, and confidence extremes. These diagnostics do not determine rank.</p></details></section>
  <section><h2>How the leaderboard combines recordings</h2><p>Counts are summed before ratios are calculated. A long recording therefore contributes more words than a short one. Selecting categories or an individual recording applies the same scorer to exactly that selection. We never average recording-level F1 scores.</p><p>Runtime pools seconds. Consumer confidence pools Quran segments. “—” means undefined or unreported. Higher is better except Unsafe green and the underlying RTF, where lower is better. The default rank uses Words F1, with equal values sharing rank.</p></section>
  <section><h2>Formulas and other speech</h2><p>Basmala and Isti'adha are evaluated separately from headline Quran word counts. In Hafs, an opening Basmala may be expressed either as Basmala or 1:1:1–1:1:4; both behave alike. When matched to al-Fatiha's opening words they count as Quran; elsewhere they are formula claims.</p><p>A Quran segment that incorrectly includes formula words is not clean. False Quran claims over formula audio reduce correctness. Quran audio labelled as a formula remains unfound. The local report includes formula occurrences detected, exact, missed, and extra. A formula is detected when more than half its words match inside one segment; an exact formula has no missing or extra words.</p><p>Null labels are allowed for non-Quran audio. Non-Quran annotations affect segment cleanliness and false-claim diagnostics.</p></section>
</article>
